input: raise KEY_MAX to 0x4ff, as on LG's webOS TV kernels

libim (the input manager inside the webOS 1.4 compositor) creates a private uinput device,
"CHECK INPUT", with UI_SET_KEYBIT for every code 0..0x4ff, and later writes the private key
code 0x4f2 to it to wake its reader and rescan the input devices. That is how devices created
after the compositor starts (lginput2's "LGE M-RCU", ajservice's "LGE AllJoyn Remote", ...)
get picked up: each daemon calls IM_RequestToCheckInput after creating its device.

With the mainline KEY_MAX of 0x2ff, UI_SET_KEYBIT fails with EINVAL at 0x300, the device is
never created, every later rescan request fails ("IM_INPUT_RequestToCheckInput ... 15(1,1266,2)
error: Invalid argument"), and keys from those devices never reach the compositor.

Only the key bitmaps grow. Userspace passes its own buffer length to EVIOCGBIT/EVIOCGKEY.

--- a/include/linux/mod_devicetable.h
+++ b/include/linux/mod_devicetable.h
@@ -333,7 +333,7 @@
 /* Input */
 #define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_EV_MAX		0x1f
 #define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_KEY_MIN_INTERESTING	0x71
-#define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_KEY_MAX		0x2ff
+#define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_KEY_MAX		0x4ff
 #define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_REL_MAX		0x0f
 #define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_ABS_MAX		0x3f
 #define INPUT_DEVICE_ID_MSC_MAX		0x07
--- a/include/uapi/linux/input-event-codes.h
+++ b/include/uapi/linux/input-event-codes.h
@@ -830,7 +830,7 @@
 
 /* We avoid low common keys in module aliases so they don't get huge. */
 #define KEY_MIN_INTERESTING	KEY_MUTE
-#define KEY_MAX			0x2ff
+#define KEY_MAX			0x4ff
 #define KEY_CNT			(KEY_MAX+1)
 
